---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'What is the most important stage in flour milling technology?',
    a: 'There is no single stage. Cleaning protects the equipment and the food-safety result; tempering controls how the wheat breaks; the flow sheet and roll setting determine extraction; sifting and purification determine flour grade. A weakness in any one stage shows up in the final ash, colour or consistency.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does a purifier always improve flour quality?',
    a: 'A purifier improves quality only when the flow sheet produces enough middlings to justify it and the operator can control the air and sieve settings. Below roughly 40 t/day, a simple mill may not generate enough controlled middlings for a purifier to earn its capital and maintenance cost.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I choose a plansifter size?',
    a: 'Start with the sifting area required per tonne of daily capacity for your flow sheet, then check the number of sections, sieve stacks and maintenance access. A plansifter that is too small limits extraction; one that is oversized adds capital and cleaning load.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does the same wheat give different extraction rates?',
    a: 'Extraction depends on wheat hardness, moisture, the flow sheet, roll condition, sifter loading and the flour grade being produced. A number from another mill is only comparable when the wheat, product specification and operating conditions are also comparable.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Where should flour quality control sit in the process?',
    a: 'Quality control is both a laboratory function and a process function. Ash, moisture, protein, granulation and farinograph results must be tied back to wheat intake, tempering, roll settings and sifter performance — otherwise the lab records a result but cannot correct the process.',
  },
];

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'CollectionPage',
    name: 'Flour Milling Technology',
    description:
      'A technical pillar on wheat cleaning, tempering, roller milling, plansifters, purifiers, extraction and flour quality control.',
    url: 'https://flourmillingpro.com/knowledge/flour-milling-technology/',
    inLanguage: 'en',
    hasPart: [
      '/knowledge/wheat-cleaning-system-design/',
      '/knowledge/wheat-cleaning-process-flow/',
      '/knowledge/dry-vs-wet-wheat-cleaning/',
      '/knowledge/wheat-tempering-moisture-guide/',
      '/knowledge/roller-mill-differential-ratio/',
      '/knowledge/roll-fluting-and-surface-technology/',
      '/knowledge/plansifter-sizing-guide/',
      '/knowledge/purifier-function-flour-milling/',
      '/knowledge/flour-extraction-rate-explained/',
      '/knowledge/flour-quality-control/',
    ].map((url) => ({ '@type': 'TechArticle', url: `https://flourmillingpro.com${url}` })),
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: f.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
    })),
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://flourmillingpro.com/' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Knowledge centre', item: 'https://flourmillingpro.com/knowledge/' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Flour Milling Technology', item: 'https://flourmillingpro.com/knowledge/flour-milling-technology/' },
    ],
  },
];
---

<PageLayout
  title="Flour Milling Technology: Cleaning, Milling & Quality"
  description="A technical pillar on wheat cleaning, tempering, roller milling, plansifters, purifiers, extraction and flour quality control."
  schema={schema}
>
  <section class="bg-steel-950">
    <div class="container-x py-14 sm:py-18">
      <p class="eyebrow-light">Pillar B · P1</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 max-w-4xl font-display text-4xl font-bold uppercase leading-[1.05] tracking-tight text-white sm:text-5xl">
        Flour milling technology: from wheat kernel to consistent flour
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-steel-300">
        Milling technology is a sequence of separations. Cleaning removes what should not enter the
        mill; tempering prepares the endosperm; rolls open and reduce; sifters and purifiers divide
        the streams; quality control proves the result. Each stage constrains the next.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-steel-400">
        For mill engineers, technical buyers and operators who need the process logic behind the equipment.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container-x max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold uppercase tracking-tight text-steel-900">The technology chain</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-steel-700">
        A change at the front of the mill rarely stays there. Wet cleaning adds water and changes
        tempering; harder wheat changes roll pressure and sifter load; a higher extraction target
        changes the purifier duty and the ash balance. The practical skill is not optimising one
        machine; it is keeping the chain in balance.
      </p>
      <div class="prose-hy mt-6">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Stage</th><th>Primary decision</th><th>What it controls</th><th>Failure symptom</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Cleaning</td><td>Dry or wet; number of stages</td><td>Impurity load, ash, equipment wear</td><td>Stones in rolls, high ash, blocked screens</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Tempering</td><td>Target moisture and time</td><td>Endosperm toughness, bran elasticity</td><td>Powdery bran, poor separation, unstable extraction</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Roller milling</td><td>Passage count, differential, flute condition</td><td>Break/reduction balance, particle size</td><td>Over-grinding, low extraction, high power</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Plansifting</td><td>Sieve area and stack configuration</td><td>Separation efficiency and circulating load</td><td>Overloaded sifter, flour in tailings</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Purification</td><td>Air, sieve and middling size</td><td>Ash separation and grade quality</td><td>Purifier bypassed or badly adjusted</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Quality control</td><td>Tests and sampling points</td><td>Product release and process correction</td><td>Lab results not linked to process changes</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section bg-steel-50">
    <div class="container-x max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold uppercase tracking-tight text-steel-900">1. Wheat cleaning technology</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-steel-700">
        Cleaning is a sequence, not a single machine. Coarse screens remove straw and large impurities;
        size grading removes small stones and broken grain; density separation removes stones that
        match the wheat in size; surface cleaning removes dirt from the crease; magnetic separation
        protects the rolls. Changing the order can blind a screen or send stones into the mill.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-steel-700">
        Read <a class="font-semibold text-ember-700" href="/knowledge/wheat-cleaning-system-design/">wheat cleaning system design</a>,
        <a class="font-semibold text-ember-700" href="/knowledge/wheat-cleaning-process-flow/">cleaning process flow</a> and
        <a class="font-semibold text-ember-700" href="/knowledge/dry-vs-wet-wheat-cleaning/">dry vs wet cleaning</a>.
        Wet cleaning improves surface cleanliness but adds water, drying load and operating cost; it is
        not automatically the better choice.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-3xl font-bold uppercase tracking-tight text-steel-900">2. Tempering and conditioning</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-steel-700">
        Tempering makes the bran tough and the endosperm friable. The target moisture depends on wheat
        class and hardness, and the time must be long enough for water to penetrate the kernel. A
        short temper at the wrong moisture produces bran that shatters into the flour and extraction
        that moves for no apparent reason.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-steel-700">
        See <a class="font-semibold text-ember-700" href="/knowledge/wheat-tempering-moisture-guide/">wheat tempering</a>
        for moisture targets, time and the effect of ±0.2%. Measure wheat moisture, not just added
        water; the starting moisture is part of the process.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-3xl font-bold uppercase tracking-tight text-steel-900">3. Roller milling technology</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-steel-700">
        Break rolls open the wheat; reduction rolls reduce the released endosperm. The differential
        ratio, flute profile, roll gap and roll condition determine how much fine material is made too
        early and how much bran is scraped into the flour. A mill can have the right number of
        passages and still perform badly because the rolls are worn or the differential is wrong for
        the duty.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-steel-700">
        Read <a class="font-semibold text-ember-700" href="/knowledge/roller-mill-differential-ratio/">roller mill differential</a>,
        <a class="font-semibold text-ember-700" href="/knowledge/roll-fluting-and-surface-technology/">roll fluting</a> and
        <a class="font-semibold text-ember-700" href="/knowledge/roll-wear-and-flute-life/">roll wear and flute life</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-3xl font-bold uppercase tracking-tight text-steel-900">4. Plansifter selection</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-steel-700">
        The plansifter separates particles by size. Its job is not merely to “sift flour”; it protects
        the reduction passages from overloading and keeps flour out of the tailings. Sifting area per
        tonne, number of sections, sieve material and maintenance access all matter.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-steel-700">
        Use <a class="font-semibold text-ember-700" href="/knowledge/plansifter-sizing-guide/">plansifter sizing</a>
        to estimate area, then check the actual stack and screen condition in the quotation.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-3xl font-bold uppercase tracking-tight text-steel-900">5. Purifier technology</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-steel-700">
        A purifier separates middlings by quality in an air stream. It is what turns a simple mill into
        a low-ash, multi-grade mill — but only if there are enough middlings to purify and an operator
        can control the air. Below a certain scale, the purifier becomes a machine that is installed
        and bypassed.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-steel-700">
        Read <a class="font-semibold text-ember-700" href="/knowledge/purifier-function-flour-milling/">what a purifier does</a>
        and why roughly 40 t/day is the practical threshold for many flow sheets.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-3xl font-bold uppercase tracking-tight text-steel-900">6. Flour extraction rate</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-steel-700">
        Extraction is the ratio of flour output to wheat input, but the number is meaningless without
        the flour grade and ash specification. Higher extraction is not automatically better: beyond
        the point where bran enters the flour, the mill is selling ash and colour problems. The
        economic optimum depends on the flour price, bran value and customer specification.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-steel-700">
        Read <a class="font-semibold text-ember-700" href="/knowledge/flour-extraction-rate-explained/">extraction definitions</a>
        and <a class="font-semibold text-ember-700" href="/knowledge/flour-extraction-rate-economics/">extraction economics</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-3xl font-bold uppercase tracking-tight text-steel-900">7. Flour quality control</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-steel-700">
        Quality control connects the finished flour to the process. Ash, moisture, protein,
        granulation and farinograph results should trigger a process question: was the wheat different,
        was tempering short, was a roll worn, was the sifter overloaded? A lab that only reports
        numbers does not control quality.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-steel-700">
        Read <a class="font-semibold text-ember-700" href="/knowledge/flour-quality-control/">flour quality control</a>,
        <a class="font-semibold text-ember-700" href="/knowledge/flour-ash-content-explained/">ash content</a> and
        <a class="font-semibold text-ember-700" href="/knowledge/flour-granulation-testing/">granulation testing</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container-x max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold uppercase tracking-tight text-steel-900">Common technology failures</h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-steel-700">
        <li><strong>Cleaning bypassed to raise throughput.</strong> The mill runs faster for a week and then pays for it in roll wear, ash and downtime.</li>
        <li><strong>Tempering treated as a timer.</strong> Moisture is added without measuring the starting wheat; extraction becomes unpredictable.</li>
        <li><strong>Flow sheet copied without wheat data.</strong> The same passages behave differently on soft, hard or high-moisture wheat.</li>
        <li><strong>Purifier installed but not operated.</strong> Capital is spent; the quality benefit never appears.</li>
        <li><strong>Quality results not tied to process.</strong> The lab reports ash after the flour is already in the silo.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-3xl font-bold uppercase tracking-tight text-steel-900">How to use this pillar</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-steel-700">
        Use the seven articles as a technical due-diligence sequence. Start with cleaning and
        tempering, because they set the condition of the wheat; then review the roll and sifter
        design; then check extraction and quality control. If a supplier cannot explain the
        interactions, the quotation is probably a machine list rather than a process design.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-6 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
        <a class="card p-4" href="/knowledge/wheat-cleaning-system-design/"><strong>Wheat cleaning system design</strong><span class="mt-1 block text-sm text-steel-600">Machine sequence and sizing.</span></a>
        <a class="card p-4" href="/knowledge/wheat-tempering-moisture-guide/"><strong>Wheat tempering</strong><span class="mt-1 block text-sm text-steel-600">Moisture, time and control.</span></a>
        <a class="card p-4" href="/knowledge/roller-mill-differential-ratio/"><strong>Roller mill differential</strong><span class="mt-1 block text-sm text-steel-600">Break vs reduction ratios.</span></a>
        <a class="card p-4" href="/knowledge/plansifter-sizing-guide/"><strong>Plansifter sizing</strong><span class="mt-1 block text-sm text-steel-600">Sifting area and stack selection.</span></a>
        <a class="card p-4" href="/knowledge/purifier-function-flour-milling/"><strong>Purifier technology</strong><span class="mt-1 block text-sm text-steel-600">Where purification earns its cost.</span></a>
        <a class="card p-4" href="/knowledge/flour-quality-control/"><strong>Flour quality control</strong><span class="mt-1 block text-sm text-steel-600">Tests, sampling and process correction.</span></a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section bg-steel-50">
    <div class="container-x max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold uppercase tracking-tight text-steel-900">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <dl class="mt-6 divide-y divide-steel-200">
        {faq.map((f) => (
          <div class="py-5 first:pt-0">
            <dt class="text-base font-bold text-steel-900">{f.q}</dt>
            <dd class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-steel-700">{f.a}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand />
</PageLayout>
